-- ================================================================
-- FIXTURE — a generated `Triage` that DECLARES ITS OWN TRUSTED PRODUCER, and
-- LOADS. A pinned gap, and the interesting thing about it is WHOSE gap it is:
-- not this example's.
--
-- IT IS THE CLASS THE PRODUCER SEALS DO NOT REACH. `Text.trusted` is gated by
-- `Permission[Vouch]`, `join_texts` is `Untrusted`-only, `render_task` declares
-- the authority it exercises, and `entity text` is `internal` — four producers,
-- four closures, each with a fixture that reds when it is undone. This program
-- needs none of them, because it writes its own:
--
--   operation launder(s: String) -> Text[Trusted]
--
-- AND NOTHING BACKS IT. No body, no host binding. The declaration is believed by
-- the typer, the call type-checks, and the program is certified.
--
-- WHY THE FIX IS NOT HERE. Body-less-and-called is the NORMAL shape in this
-- example: twenty-one operations in `lib/` are declared with no body, and every
-- accepted fixture calls some of them — `Email.fetch`, `observe`,
-- `choose_recipient`, `render_task`, `complete`. A spec DECLARES and a deployment
-- BINDS; the test harness binds five names and a real deployment would bind the
-- rest. So "body-less" cannot be an error, and nothing available at load separates
-- a declaration awaiting its binding from one asserting a type its author cannot
-- produce. No amount of sealing in `lib/vocabulary.anthill` reaches a name the
-- candidate invents.
--
-- WHAT WOULD REACH IT is a KERNEL check that a CALLED body-less operation has some
-- implementation — a body, a defining equation, or a host binding — by the end of
-- the load. WI-1122 put the host-fn table on the KB BEFORE `load_all` precisely so
-- that late registration could be refused, so the information is there. Under such
-- a check this file stops loading, and that is the right outcome: the program is
-- not safe-and-unrunnable, it is ill-formed. FILED AS WI-20260908-FJG8B, whose
-- acceptance names this fixture: when it lands, this file becomes REFUSED and
-- should be deleted rather than repaired.
--
-- UNTIL THEN IT IS INERT, and for a reason that is an accident rather than a
-- property: nothing runs a `Triage`. The pipeline generates and CHECKS; `run_triage`
-- is `run`'s only caller and only a test drives it, so the missing implementation is
-- never reached and no bytes move. Recorded because the checker's verdict is
-- nevertheless wrong — it certifies a program resting on a signature nothing backs
-- — and because the day the kernel check lands, this row is what notices.
--
-- NOT A REGRESSION IN THE TRUST DESIGN, though it is one in verdict: the previous
-- `requires releasable(body)` on `Email.send` happened to refuse this program, since
-- `releasable(launder(…))` is nobody's row. That was the sink catching an
-- ill-formed program as a side effect of checking content, not the lattice working.
-- WI-20260829-MCKTE.
-- ================================================================
sort guardians.agent.DeclaredMintTriage
  import anthill.prelude.{String, List, Error, External}
  import anthill.prelude.List.{mapElems}
  import guardians.{Triage, Email, Mailbox, Report, Address, Text, Llm, summarize, Verdict, observe}
  import guardians.TrustLevel.{Trusted}
  entity mk

  operation launder(s: String) -> Text[Trusted]

  operation run(self: DeclaredMintTriage, box: Mailbox, llm: Llm,
                wording: Text[Trusted]) -> Report
    ensures mentions_all(result, box)
    effects {External, llm.E, Error} =
      let msgs = Email.fetch(box)
      let sent = Email.send(to:   Address(local: "boss", domain: "ourcorp.com"),
                            body: launder(box.owner.local))
      Report(items:   mapElems[EffP = {llm.E, Error}](msgs,
                        lambda m -> Verdict(message:    m.id,
                                            evidence:   mapElems(observe(llm, m), lambda o -> o.feature))),
             summary: summarize(llm, wording, msgs.map(lambda m -> m.body).collect()))

  provides Triage[C = DeclaredMintTriage]
end
